1
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:12,900
In this video, we want to talk about storage area network, or SAN technologies.

2
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:18,080
And a SAN is a way to put a storage device on our network

3
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:23,360
and let devices on our network, like servers, see that storage as being locally attached.

4
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:27,540
And we want to talk about a few different options of having storage on our network.

5
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:30,460
Here in this example, we've got some independent servers.

6
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:31,940
We've got server 1, server 2.

7
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:35,160
They each have a connection to their own hard drives.

8
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:39,280
This direct connection to their hard drives, which might be internal hard drives,

9
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:42,760
this is called DAS, D-A-S, direct attached storage.

10
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:45,880
And typically, servers are going to be running SCSI.

11
00:00:46,260 --> 00:00:49,240
SCSI stands for small computer system interface.

12
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:54,120
And that's the language that a server or computer can speak to its hard drive.

13
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:58,460
And when this server is accessing information from the hard drive over SCSI,

14
00:00:58,980 --> 00:01:01,540
it's using what's referred to as block-level storage.

15
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:03,840
Block-level storage is very efficient,

16
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:09,020
because instead of trying to retrieve or send entire files at a time,

17
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:11,280
we're dealing down at a low level.

18
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:12,420
We're dealing with bytes and bits.

19
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:13,700
We're dealing with a block of data.

20
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:18,460
So block-level storage is more efficient than file-level storage.

21
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:20,340
When we think of file-level storage,

22
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:23,720
think of something like having a file server on a network.

23
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:26,480
Maybe on Microsoft Windows, you click on Start, Run,

24
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:28,400
and then type in backslash, backslash,

25
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:29,980
and you give the server name and a backslash,

26
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:31,780
and you give the shared resource.

27
00:01:31,780 --> 00:01:33,820
And that's going to give you a window,

28
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:36,160
where you can start browsing files on this server.

29
00:01:36,580 --> 00:01:40,840
Well, that computer is seeing that server as external to itself.

30
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:42,620
It's not seeing it as local storage.

31
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:45,160
And it's going to be using file-level access,

32
00:01:45,340 --> 00:01:47,020
as opposed to block-level access.

33
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:48,600
And it's not quite as efficient.

34
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:52,360
And something that's not very optimal with this DAS solution is,

35
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:56,200
let's say that server 2 has extra space on their hard drive.

36
00:01:56,580 --> 00:01:58,660
There's not a really easy way for server 1

37
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:02,040
to use some of that space that server 2 has available.

38
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:03,620
One fix for that issue,

39
00:02:03,620 --> 00:02:07,000
is to put a storage device on the network.

40
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:10,160
It's called a NAS, a network-attached storage device.

41
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:14,280
And this network appliance acts a lot like a file server.

42
00:02:14,620 --> 00:02:16,120
We access it like a file server.

43
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:20,000
It's using file-level storage, as opposed to block-level.

44
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:22,460
And everything is Ethernet at this point.

45
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:24,540
This makes it really convenient for servers,

46
00:02:24,660 --> 00:02:28,600
and maybe even PCs, to share a common repository of data.

47
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:31,700
However, because we're using this file-level storage,

48
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:33,600
the performance is just not going to be as good as it is now.

49
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:37,800
It's not going to be there as compared to having a directly attached SCSI device.

50
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:41,020
However, we can get some dramatic performance improvements

51
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:43,760
by using something called Fiber Channel.

52
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:48,020
And you see this in lots of large enterprise storage area networks.

53
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:51,800
Fiber Channel does allow us to do block-level storage.

54
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:54,140
Much more efficient than file-level storage.

55
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:57,000
So in this case, we've got Ethernet up at the top of the screen,

56
00:02:57,380 --> 00:02:59,760
and we've got Fiber Channel down at the bottom of the screen.

57
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:03,420
Now, notice the servers have a couple of connections going out.

58
00:03:03,740 --> 00:03:04,980
For example, consider server 2.

59
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:08,360
It's connecting to the Ethernet switch using a NIC, a network interface card.

60
00:03:08,740 --> 00:03:11,280
But how does it connect to the Fiber Channel switch?

61
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:15,060
Well, it uses something called an HBAA host bus adapter.

62
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:17,420
And this is going to create additional expense.

63
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:19,600
It's going to create additional wiring requirements.

64
00:03:20,060 --> 00:03:21,460
We've got to have two connections out.

65
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:23,480
One for the LAN, and one for the SAN.

66
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:28,580
But Fiber Channel does give us very high-speed access to our data, which is great.

67
00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:32,640
However, a technology that's really getting popular these days in larger enterprises

68
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:33,580
is something called...

69
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:36,200
Fiber Channel over Ethernet.

70
00:03:36,620 --> 00:03:38,660
We still get the benefit of Fiber Channel.

71
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:40,760
We still get to do block-level storage.

72
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:45,360
However, notice here the servers don't have to have a NIC and an HBAA.

73
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:47,380
They just connect into this Ethernet switch.

74
00:03:47,720 --> 00:03:52,200
And we're Ethernet until we get to this FCOE, this Fiber Channel over Ethernet switch.

75
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:57,360
And then we go Fiber Channel from that FCOE switch down to the Fiber Channel storage.

76
00:03:57,700 --> 00:04:00,260
This is a nice compromise that helps us save on expense.

77
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:02,020
We don't have to buy an HBAA for the servers.

78
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:03,580
We don't have to have that additional...

79
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:07,860
cable going from the server to the Fiber Channel switch.

80
00:04:08,260 --> 00:04:09,340
And we get high-speed throughput.

81
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:13,140
But still, this is more expensive than another alternative out there.

82
00:04:13,460 --> 00:04:17,800
You typically see Fiber Channel over Ethernet in large enterprise deployments.

83
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:23,700
But for small to medium-sized deployments, you might instead see something called iSCSI,

84
00:04:24,220 --> 00:04:27,120
which is Internet Small Computer System Interface.

85
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:29,760
With iSCSI, there is no Fiber Channel.

86
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:31,360
Here's Server 1, Server 2.

87
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:32,780
They're connected into an Ethernet switch.

88
00:04:32,780 --> 00:04:36,460
And so is an iSCSI storage device.

89
00:04:36,980 --> 00:04:38,840
Everything is running over IP.

90
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:43,860
We're encapsulating those SCSI messages and data inside of IP packets.

91
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:49,480
And because we are speaking SCSI, it's just over IP, we get to do block-level access.

92
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:53,420
And this is a huge cost savings as opposed to doing something with Fiber Channel.

93
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:57,560
But technically, the performance is not quite as good as Fiber Channel over Ethernet.

94
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:00,740
But the expense is much less than Fiber Channel over Ethernet.

95
00:05:00,740 --> 00:05:02,340
And that's a few of the SAN.

96
00:05:02,340 --> 00:05:05,160
And the storage area network options that we have available today.
